What are Props?
Props (Properties) are used to pass data from a parent component to a child component.

Think of props as:
Function arguments for components
Read-only data
A way to make components reusable

## Key Idea
Parent Component  ➜  sends data (props)  ➜  Child Component receives data
#Basic Example

^^Parent Component (App.jsx)

import React from "react";                    //Example for how to use it
import Card from "./Card";

const App = () => {
  return (
    <>
      <Card name="Hemel" age={20} />
      <Card name="John" age={25} />
      <Card name="Sara" age={22} />
    </>
  );
};

export default App;

^^Child Component (Card.jsx)
import React from "react";                  //Example of how to use on child 

const Card = (props) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Name: {props.name}</h2>
      <p>Age: {props.age}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Card;

Cleaner Way (Destructuring Props)                            //Best way to use it.
Instead of props.name, we extract values directly:

const Card = ({ name, age }) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Name: {name}</h2>
      <p>Age: {age}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Card;

How Props Work Internally
1. Parent defines data
2. Data is passed as attributes
3. Child receives data as function parameter
4. React renders UI using that data

Real-Life Example
<UserCard name="iPhone 15" price={1200} />
<UserCard name="Samsung S24" price={1100} />

Same component → different data → reusable UI

## Rules of Props
✔ Props are read-only (cannot be changed in child)
✔ Props flow ONLY from parent → child
✔ Props make components reusable
✔ Props can be string, number, array, object, function

Common Mistakes
Changing props inside child component
Forgetting to pass props
Using props without {}
Confusing props with state

Props:
→ Passed from parent
→ Cannot be changed
→ External data


Props = “Data coming from outside”

Final Understanding
Props = way to pass data to components so they become reusable and dynamic

One-line summary
 “Props are how React components talk to each other.”


